      },
      "supplement_materials": [
        {
          "auxiliary_material": "The study employed landmark analysis to minimize immortal time bias in irAE assessment and validated efficacy-related metabolite findings in external cohorts (Routy, McCulloch). The discussion proposes a 'smart' fecal microbiota transplantation concept based on strain dynamics and antigen mimicry profiles. The study design was a non-randomized, unblinded, prospective cohort with median follow-up of 1167 days.",
          "evidence": [],
          "gap_id": "Ch6_gap_5",
          "judgment_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\planning\\chapters\\external\\N4452dfb25f491e9335fe-5a6b848383c152a3\\round_2\\supplements\\Ch6_gap_5\\attempt-u2gi65cq\\source_units\\SU01_search_candidate_4e312d3d88\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json",
          "limitations": [],
          "material_ready": true,
          "material_usefulness": "supporting",
          "outline_action": "Use this study to summarize current mechanistic hypotheses for irAEs (molecular mimicry, SCFA depletion, strain variation) and to cite as an example of existing literature that, despite mechanistic depth, remains limited by small sample sizes and composite irAE grouping. This supports the argument that large-scale, subtype-specific studies are still needed to validate findings for non-colitis irAEs.",
          "outline_action_recovered_by_policy": false,
          "remaining_gap": "The specific request for large-scale microbiome association studies focused on non-colitis irAEs (such as myocarditis or neurotoxicity) is not fulfilled by this material. While this study includes some non-colitis irAE cases (e.g., cardiovascular, muscle) within a composite 'severe irAE' group, the total sample size (n=13 for severe irAE) is small, and there is no dedicated large-scale analysis for specific non-colitis subtypes. The authors' own admission of limited sample size confirms the ongoing evidence gap regarding statistical power for specific irAE subtypes.",
          "remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy": false,
          "status": "unmet",
          "useful_material": "This study investigated gut microbiome and metabolome associations with long-term ICI outcomes and immune-related adverse events (irAEs) in a prospective cohort of lung cancer patients (metagenome n=54, metabolome n=49). The authors grouped patients into benefit, severe irAE, and resistance categories. The severe irAE group (n=13 in metagenome cohort) included various irAE types such as cardiovascular toxicity (2 cases), muscle toxicity (1 case), endocrine, skin, and gastrointestinal toxicities, rather than focusing exclusively on colitis. Mechanistically, the authors reported that microbiota sequences from irAE patients aligned with autoimmune antigenic epitopes (molecular mimicry), particularly noting SLE-related epitopes enriched in colitis patients, and that beneficial outcomes correlated with higher short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) like butyrate and propionate. Strain-level variations in Escherichia coli and Faecalibacterium prausnitzii were also associated with clinical phenotypes. The authors explicitly stated in the limitations that the sample size was relatively small due to strict quality control and long-term follow-up requirements."
        }
